---
import { journey, sectionNo } from '../data/site'
import Reg from './Reg.astro'
---

{/*
  THE HANDOFF — the single largest gesture on the page (8 on the intensity
  budget), and the thing that makes the cream-to-dark change feel authored
  rather than abrupt.

  Scrubbed entirely by scroll across 200vh:
    0.00–0.45  the last rule of Capabilities curves downward       TRACE
    0.30–0.75  the dark ground rises behind it                     REVEAL
    0.72–0.92  the title arrives, once the ground has landed       REVEAL

  The curve does not stop at the bottom of the stage: it becomes the vertical
  route the eras below are pinned to, so one line carries you from one section
  into the next.

  The path below is only the no-JS fallback. The rail it has to land on sits at
  the page gutter, which is a clamp() on the viewport width, so no constant in
  a fixed viewBox can hit it — with the motion runtime the geometry is measured
  and this `d` is rewritten in pixel space (layoutCurve, in motion/scroll.ts).
  These coordinates are a close approximation for the static case.
*/}
{/* NOT aria-hidden: this carries the Journey heading, and hiding the section
    would remove that heading from the accessibility tree entirely. Only the
    decorative plate and curve are hidden. */}
<section class="handoff" data-handoff>
  <div class="handoff__stage">
    <div class="handoff__plate" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <svg class="handoff__curve" viewBox="0 0 1000 600" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <path
        d="M0 300 H420 C580 300 80 480 80 600"
        vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
      />
      <path
        class="is-route"
        data-fam="trace"
        data-draw
        d="M0 300 H420 C580 300 80 480 80 600"
        vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"
      />

      {/* The head of the line, riding the draw. The stage is pinned for most
          of the scrub, which means nothing on screen moves except what is
          being scrubbed — a stroke lengthening by a few pixels a frame was
          not enough to register as movement, and the section read as a still
          image you were scrolling past. The node is placed by
          getPointAtLength in motion/scroll.ts. */}
      <g class="handoff__node" data-handoff-node>
        <circle class="handoff__node-ring" r="13" />
        <circle class="handoff__node-dot" r="3.5" />
      </g>
    </svg>

    {/*
      The two ends of the curve, named. Without them the line is a shape; with
      them it is a statement about where you have been and where this is going,
      which is the only reason the gesture is on the page.

      Cyan rather than ink: they are printed over BOTH grounds — the sheet
      before the wipe and the plate after it — and cyan is the one value in the
      system that holds on either. Placed from --entry-y / --rail-x, which
      layoutCurve writes after measuring the same geometry the path uses, so a
      label can never drift off the line it is labelling.
    */}
    <p class="handoff__mark handoff__mark--from" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>From</span> Capabilities
    </p>
    <p class="handoff__mark handoff__mark--to" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>To</span> Journey
    </p>

    <div class="handoff__title">
      <p class="eyebrow handoff__eyebrow">{sectionNo('journey')} / 2020 — 2026</p>
      <h2 class="section-title display" id="journey-heading">
        <Reg text="Six years, four shipped." />
      </h2>
      <p class="handoff__caption">
        The line does not stop here. Every period below is pinned to it, and it
        draws as you read them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/*
  This section IS a sequence — the periods are consecutive and each depends on
  the one before — so it is the only part of the site that keeps an ordered
  structure, and it uses the real period labels rather than step numbers.

  The route draws as you read and each marker turns cyan as it is passed, so
  the section reports progress instead of decorating it. Past eras dim, the
  one you are reading is full strength, and what is still ahead stays faint.

  No DRIFT ghost here, unlike About. The word behind About is "Constraint" -
  it names what the section is about. The one here was "2020", which is not a
  word but a year, and one that is already printed twice in the list beside
  it; at ghost opacity it read as a stray number rather than as atmosphere.
*/}
<section
  class="journey chapter shell is-plate"
  id="journey"
  data-chapter
  data-plate
  aria-labelledby="journey-heading"
>
  {/* The rail is a sibling of the list, not a child of it — an <ol> may only
      contain <li>, and the marker positions depend on sharing this box. */}
  <div class="journey__eras">
    <span class="journey__route" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>

    <ol>
      {journey.map((era) => (
        <li class="era" data-state="future">
          <span class="era__marker" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="era__period">{era.period}</p>
          <div>
            <h3 class="era__title">{era.title}</h3>
            <p class="era__body">{era.body}</p>
            {era.coda && <p class="era__coda">{era.coda}</p>}
            <ul class="era__tags">
              {era.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
